      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans, and the fluids of all known living organisms. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a molecule made up of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n* Water is unique because of how these atoms bond. It is a **polar molecule**, meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity acts like tiny magnets, causing water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion). This is why water forms droplets and has high surface tension.\n\n### 2. The Physical View (States of Matter)\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature, found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water (ice) is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water heats up and evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View (The Source of Life)\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property makes it essential for life:\n* **In Biology:** The human body is made of about 60% water. It acts as a transport medium, carrying nutrients and oxygen to cells, regulating body temperature, and flushing out waste.\n* **On Earth:** Water covers about 71% of the Earth's surface, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of Earth's water). It drives global weather patterns, shapes landscapes, and sustains every known ecosystem.\n\nIn short, water is the fundamental elixir of life on Earth—simple in"
